1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,560
At least 50 million of them lie embedded in forests, rice paddies, deserts, and ocean surf.

2
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:16,100
They can blow up a battleship, a tank, a platoon, a soldier, but mostly they blow up civilians.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,420
Now, Landmines on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,120
Since World War II, an estimated 400 million mines have been deployed on land and sea.

5
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:50,160
In war after war, mines have played a major and sometimes critical role,

6
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:52,800
adding a new dimension to modern combat

7
00:00:52,801 --> 00:00:58,360
and establishing themselves as a staple for armies and navies, both big and small.

8
00:00:58,361 --> 00:01:09,860
This is a very viable weapon system for all times and all seasons and can be used to not just shape the battlefield, but to have a great psychological effect.

9
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:22,900
If you have a cheap $100 mine, you have 10 of them and you blow up 10 tanks, maybe that's 30 or 40 million dollars.

10
00:01:22,901 --> 00:01:32,660
So on a cost basis, you can see where the landmine is a nice cheap way of defeating a high-tech enemy.

11
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:38,900
They are a useful military tool, and otherwise there never would have been so many mines.

12
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:45,180
The problem is that post-conflict, dumb anti-personnel mines are going to affect civilian populations.

13
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:48,480
And that's a problem. It's a serious problem.

14
00:01:48,481 --> 00:01:58,980
That problem has escalated exponentially in recent decades, as mines have become a weapon of choice on all sides.

15
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:05,980
Rebel groups depend on mines as a cheap way of knocking out big weapons and establishing a presence.

16
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:16,560
And superpowers see mines as a force multiplier, a surrogate soldier that can reduce the need for large armies and thereby save military lives.

17
00:02:16,561 --> 00:02:23,760
As a result, some 700 different kinds of land and sea mines have been strewn across vast regions of the planet.

18
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:33,320
Unfortunately, as weapons of chaos and terror, mines do their worst damage long after the fighting is done.

19
00:02:33,321 --> 00:02:39,960
They send a blast wave up through your foot into your leg,

20
00:02:39,961 --> 00:02:44,880
and the tissues around the bone explode off the bone,

21
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:49,880
causing damage way up inside the skin and inside the muscle.

22
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:51,060
It's really unspeakable.

23
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:57,200
Actually seeing what landmines do, it's something that you can't handle.

24
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:02,240
You can't handle the truth of this disastrous weapon.

25
00:03:02,241 --> 00:03:09,100
or the culpability that comes from knowing that our tax dollars maim children.

26
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:20,960
Since 1975, more than 500,000 civilians, half of them children, have been killed or maimed by landmines.

27
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:27,080
Tens of millions of live mines remain buried in 70 countries around the world,

28
00:03:27,081 --> 00:03:33,080
making daily life a game of Russian roulette for two-thirds of the world's poorest nations.

29
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:37,320
Mines stay deadly because they're so simple.

30
00:03:38,160 --> 00:03:42,980
The vast majority consist of a fuse that shoots off a spark when stepped on or driven over,

31
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:49,060
setting off a small amount of initiating explosive, which in turn detonates the main charge.

32
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:52,440
As long as the fuse is intact, the mine is alive.

33
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:56,680
How did the planet ever get this way?

34
00:03:57,080 --> 00:04:02,400
Its life-giving soil and sea, riddled with so many pockets of mutilation and death.

35
00:04:05,660 --> 00:04:09,800
The origin of landmines goes back to the 13th century and China.

36
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:16,580
It only took about 100 years after the invention of gunpowder for the Chinese to create the world's first exploding mine,

37
00:04:17,180 --> 00:04:19,680
called the underground, sky-soaring thunder.

38
00:04:20,980 --> 00:04:26,000
Flags, lances, and other attractive items were set in the ground to entice enemy horsemen.

39
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:30,860
When one was pulled, it would trigger an igniter attached to the gunpowder charge.

40
00:04:32,140 --> 00:04:36,560
Armies throughout Europe tried for centuries to fine-tune the potentially devastating new weapon.

41
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:41,700
But it was an American, a Confederate general by the name of Gabriel Rains,

42
00:04:41,701 --> 00:04:46,740
who first put landmines to widespread practical use during the American Civil War.

43
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:52,580
We literally had thousands used in the defense of Richmond in 1864,

44
00:04:53,020 --> 00:04:54,580
and they claimed hundreds of casualties.

45
00:04:54,581 --> 00:05:02,360
There were even anti-vehicle mines where Confederate cavalry and partisans would place anti-vehicle mines on railroad tracks.

46
00:05:02,540 --> 00:05:05,620
There's indications they took out eight or more trains in Tennessee.

47
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:14,360
Anti-vehicle mines packed with 20 or more pounds of explosives were the precursors to the modern anti-tank mine.

48
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:21,480
But Reins also deployed smaller mines aimed at individual soldiers, anti-personnel mines.

49
00:05:21,481 --> 00:05:33,860
In fact, the first landmine casualties of the Civil War were inflicted by an anti-personnel mine, which injured a Union scout and killed his horse on May 4, 1862.

50
00:05:37,140 --> 00:05:43,480
Rains' rudimentary mines were made in the field from artillery shells that he fused with both pressure and tripwire mechanisms.

51
00:05:45,880 --> 00:05:48,300
The pressure mines went off when stepped on.

52
00:05:48,301 --> 00:05:53,300
Trip wires detonated the mine when rushed by an arm or leg.

53
00:05:53,301 --> 00:06:01,300
Rings' pioneering fuse consisted of a mix of volatile chemicals in ground glass covered by a thin copper cap.

54
00:06:01,301 --> 00:06:10,300
When stepped on, the compound ignited, setting off explosives in the ignition channel, which would then detonate the main charge.

55
00:06:10,301 --> 00:06:16,300
At first the fuses were so sensitive, the mines went off virtually on contact.

56
00:06:16,301 --> 00:06:23,300
After losing a thumb and forefinger to one of his own mines, however, Rain set the pressure point for detonation at seven pounds.

57
00:06:23,301 --> 00:06:30,300
Overall, about 20,000 mines were deployed during the war, killing a few hundred soldiers.

58
00:06:30,301 --> 00:06:37,300
From the start, land mines were viewed with abhorrence, at least by the North.

59
00:06:37,301 --> 00:06:42,880
This was not the proper way of conducting war.

60
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:47,640
To be blown up by something you can't see doesn't seem fair.

61
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:52,120
But the new weapon once in play proved compelling.

62
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:56,520
And by the end of the war, the North, too, was using landmines.

63
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:05,860
Half a century later in the First World War, a whole new raft of unfair weapons would be introduced.

64
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:12,880
Everything from poison gas to machine guns to the armored tank.

65
00:07:14,800 --> 00:07:18,240
The tank in particular would push the development of landmines.

66
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,320
The Germans take artillery shells, much as Reigns had done,

67
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:29,820
bury them with forms of friction primers and percussion primers

68
00:07:29,821 --> 00:07:34,860
as the means of trying to stop the new weapon on the battlefield called the tank.

69
00:07:34,861 --> 00:07:41,860
German mines were improvised in the field using a wood or metal box to hold either a shell or an explosive powder.

70
00:07:41,861 --> 00:07:47,860
The pressure fuses were designed to trigger the mines under the weight of a tank or field gun.

71
00:07:47,861 --> 00:07:55,860
Some of the mines were booby-trapped to explode if and when the box was opened or moved by enemy deminers.

72
00:07:55,861 --> 00:08:03,860
Since tanks didn't appear until late in the war, anti-tank mines did not take center stage as a major weapon.

73
00:08:03,861 --> 00:08:05,081
a major weapon.

74
00:08:05,082 --> 00:08:10,860
Still, the mines made a deep impression and were responsible for 16% of

75
00:08:10,861 --> 00:08:17,240
all American tank losses.

76
00:08:17,241 --> 00:08:21,619
Tanks and anti-tank mines continued to square off after the war, as these U.S.

77
00:08:21,620 --> 00:08:22,320
Army tests

78
00:08:22,321 --> 00:08:23,540
of 1926 show.

79
00:08:23,541 --> 00:08:31,180
But peacetime reconstruction eventually put mine research on hold, except

80
00:08:31,181 --> 00:08:38,180
Germany, which began gearing up for war soon after Hitler came to power in 1933.

81
00:08:38,181 --> 00:08:44,180
In the history of mine warfare, World War II was a watershed.

82
00:08:44,181 --> 00:08:50,180
On every front, in astonishing numbers, with horrifying innovations and designs,

83
00:08:50,181 --> 00:08:59,180
landmines took center stage, establishing themselves as both fixture and symbol of 20th century war.

84
00:08:59,181 --> 00:09:09,939
Germany, far ahead of everyone in the design and use of landmines, introduced a new mine every three months of the war, baffling the Allies with anti-handling devices,

85
00:09:09,940 --> 00:09:15,480
new fuses, and other refinements that made the mines more deadly and harder to detect.

86
00:09:15,481 --> 00:09:24,160
Anti-personnel mines were used to keep enemy soldiers from removing anti-tank mines,

87
00:09:24,161 --> 00:09:29,560
and both were laid out in minefields, mathematically designed to produce the most casualties.

88
00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:34,320
You need to have the mines spaced closely enough

89
00:09:34,321 --> 00:09:40,120
that a vehicle or a person can't get through without activating a mine.

90
00:09:40,121 --> 00:09:47,200
But you can't space them so close together that the detonation of one causes a detonation of another.

91
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:51,640
The Allies quickly learned from the Germans,

92
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:57,740
developing mine-laying drills in which squads of engineers would bury mines at carefully measured distances

93
00:09:57,741 --> 00:10:02,480
and camouflage the mines with great care.

94
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:05,720
Most of the mines in the war were pressure mines,

95
00:10:05,721 --> 00:10:12,120
But tripwires, which extended the effective range of the mine by many feet, were also deployed.

96
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:24,128
The most shocking new anti-personnel mine of the war was undoubtedly the German S-Mine, short for Shrapnel Mine, otherwise known to American troops as the Bouncing

97
00:10:24,129 --> 00:10:24,829
Betty.

98
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:31,160
Introduced against the French in 1940, the mine was activated by tripwire or footfall.

99
00:10:31,700 --> 00:10:32,840
But then the surprise.

100
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:38,200
Instead of exploding from below, a small charge of black powder shot the mine belly high.

101
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:43,720
Then the main charge exploded, sending 350 steel balls in all directions.

102
00:10:45,240 --> 00:10:51,020
It's the first anti-personnel mine designed to kill large numbers of people.

103
00:10:51,200 --> 00:10:57,720
It's got a frag pattern with a lethal radius of about 25 meters in a 360-degree pattern.

104
00:10:58,060 --> 00:11:00,940
So it was possible for an S-mine to kill or wound an entire squad.

105
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:12,160
Many of the wounded and dead were combat engineers, who had the nerve-wracking job of sweeping metal detectors across every inch of front in search of mines.

106
00:11:12,161 --> 00:11:23,160
That job would become much more hazardous mid-war, as the Germans introduced a new mine, one that none of the Allied metal detecting tools could find.

107
00:11:23,161 --> 00:11:30,040
One out of every 236 Cambodian civilians has lost a limb by stepping on a landmine.

108
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:34,040
Landmines will return on Modern Marvels.

109
00:11:38,040 --> 00:11:47,380
For the contemporary combat engineer, advances in minesweeping technology haven't improved dramatically since World War II.

110
00:11:47,381 --> 00:11:58,380
The critical weapons are the metal detector, the probe, and a whole lot of caution and nerve.

111
00:11:58,381 --> 00:12:06,380
Metal detectors weren't nearly as reliable in World War II, but they still provided the last and best line of defense against stepping on a mine.

112
00:12:06,381 --> 00:12:12,880
When you're using a detector, your life, in a way, depends upon it.

113
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:19,360
And you sweep carefully, and you make sure you sweep thoroughly.

114
00:12:20,620 --> 00:12:28,860
Jack Wack was a 21-year-old combat engineer assigned to lay in clear mines during the Allied advance through Italy in 1944.

115
00:12:28,861 --> 00:12:39,780
When you're sweeping and you get a signal, you immediately freeze and gradually ease to the ground.

116
00:12:40,380 --> 00:12:47,760
And you gradually start probing horizontally until you feel some metal.

117
00:12:48,360 --> 00:12:58,380
Now, once you find it, you gradually, gradually take away the dirt until you expose the top of it.

118
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:04,640
And then you try to look for any trip wires that they booby-trapped the thing.

119
00:13:05,080 --> 00:13:10,060
And then comes the time when you pull it out.

120
00:13:11,180 --> 00:13:15,820
The mine that gave combat engineers the most trouble was the German Schumann,

121
00:13:16,380 --> 00:13:21,320
a non-metal mine introduced in 1942 that metal detectors couldn't pick up.

122
00:13:21,321 --> 00:13:31,200
You couldn't find the shoe mine, which was a small wooden box and had only one little piece of metal in the whole thing.

123
00:13:32,500 --> 00:13:38,560
With those, when we were searching for mines, that was the single thing we dreaded.

124
00:13:39,720 --> 00:13:45,000
Wack lost both legs when he stepped on a shoe mine while clearing debris around a bombed-out bridge.

125
00:13:46,360 --> 00:13:50,140
When I stepped on the mine, I didn't realize it.

126
00:13:50,141 --> 00:13:55,640
There was no indication by the needle. There was no sound.

127
00:13:56,560 --> 00:14:01,160
And the first thing I knew was the tremendous roar, and I was up in the air.

128
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:05,760
I knew it was a shoe mine because just from the amount of the explosion,

129
00:14:06,300 --> 00:14:14,040
and when I laid down and looked, I could see my legs, and I could see just the feet were blown off.

130
00:14:14,041 --> 00:14:18,980
I sat there a second, and then I felt this sense of peace.

131
00:14:20,140 --> 00:14:27,640
I was completely confident that even though I lost two legs, I would lead a normal life.

132
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:34,300
The dozer operator carried me out, and they called for an ambulance.

133
00:14:34,660 --> 00:14:38,240
In about a half hour, an ambulance came and took me away.

134
00:14:40,480 --> 00:14:43,240
If the Germans had the upper hand in laying mines,

135
00:14:43,241 --> 00:14:48,240
the Allies, by necessity, came up with the best tools for rapidly breaching minefields.

136
00:14:50,140 --> 00:14:57,140
Pounding the ground with flails that had enough armor to protect the operator while detonating mines was one way.

137
00:14:57,141 --> 00:15:02,140
Rollers could also be used to set off mines.

138
00:15:02,141 --> 00:15:08,140
And plows were employed to clear a path by shoveling mines off to the side.

139
00:15:08,141 --> 00:15:19,140
Explosive snakes pushed hundreds of yards across a minefield and then blown up to breach a path wide enough for a tank also saw some action.

140
00:15:19,141 --> 00:15:24,060
as did a smaller line charge called the Bangalore torpedo.

141
00:15:25,900 --> 00:15:29,480
The squad pushes the pipe forward across the minefield.

142
00:15:29,780 --> 00:15:32,100
Then they put the explosive train in the back.

143
00:15:32,500 --> 00:15:35,400
They detonate it, and it detonates the mines that it's up against.

144
00:15:35,401 --> 00:15:39,400
That explosive-to-explosive continuity causes the mines to detonate.

145
00:15:41,480 --> 00:15:46,780
One of the most important mine-countermine showdowns of the war took place on D-Day.

146
00:15:47,540 --> 00:15:53,120
When American and British troops hit the beaches of Normandy, the first line of German defense was mines.

147
00:15:53,980 --> 00:15:56,800
Four million of them along the Atlantic coast.

148
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:04,460
Most were German teller mines, anti-tank pressure mines containing 12 pounds of TNT.

149
00:16:05,820 --> 00:16:11,220
Grommel would put wooden sticks in the ground and then attach a teller mine to the top of it,

150
00:16:11,221 --> 00:16:18,780
with the idea being that at certain tide levels, the landing craft would come in, hit the teller mine,

151
00:16:18,980 --> 00:16:20,940
it would go off and destroy the landing craft.

152
00:16:21,920 --> 00:16:28,100
One of the things not usually appreciated about D-Day is that American engineers constituted 40 to 50 percent of the initial landing effort.

153
00:16:28,380 --> 00:16:29,940
So it was a major effort.

154
00:16:29,941 --> 00:16:33,620
And one of the reasons we had so many problems on Omaha is we didn't get many of the lanes open

155
00:16:33,621 --> 00:16:38,500
so that we could then land tanks and other heavy equipment to help us deal with the defenses.

156
00:16:38,940 --> 00:16:40,180
We suffered heavy casualties.

157
00:16:41,220 --> 00:16:44,200
About a year after D-Day, World War II was over.

158
00:16:50,120 --> 00:16:57,300
But just a few years later, mines were once again being put into the earth in large numbers, this time in Korea.

159
00:16:58,720 --> 00:17:02,700
Korea's craggy, mountainous terrain made anti-tank mines much more efficient,

160
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:06,420
since tanks were more or less forced to go along predictable paths.

161
00:17:06,421 --> 00:17:09,990
Just 80 anti-tank mines were used for every U.S.

162
00:17:09,991 --> 00:17:15,420
tank destroyed, compared to 2,000 mines per tank in the Second World War.

163
00:17:18,420 --> 00:17:26,884
Anti-personnel mines weren't so effective, at least not as a deterrent to enemy movement, since the Koreans were willing to use human wave tactics to breach a

164
00:17:26,885 --> 00:17:27,585
minefield.

165
00:17:27,586 --> 00:17:30,867
reach a minefield. As a result, the U.S.

166
00:17:30,868 --> 00:17:35,780
developed a new kind of mine, a directional fragmentation

167
00:17:35,781 --> 00:17:39,237
mine, that would make such assaults completely suicidal.

168
00:17:39,238 --> 00:17:41,460
The mining equivalent of a sawed-off

169
00:17:41,461 --> 00:17:49,500
shotgun, the Claymore mine could be fired by tripwire or electrically by remote control.

170
00:17:50,840 --> 00:17:56,280
It could spew 700 steel balls in whatever direction it was aimed, with a wounding range

171
00:17:56,281 --> 00:18:02,500
of 50 yards. Being able to aim the mine made it a much more efficient weapon.

172
00:18:03,760 --> 00:18:08,869
You can point it in the direction that your enemy is coming from.

173
00:18:08,870 --> 00:18:10,520
A bouncing Betty will

174
00:18:10,521 --> 00:18:16,214
spread shrapnel in 360 degree arc, so it will shoot shrapnel everywhere.

175
00:18:16,215 --> 00:18:17,400
The directional

176
00:18:17,401 --> 00:18:21,320
mine, theoretically, the bad stuff only goes toward the enemy.

177
00:18:21,321 --> 00:18:28,320
Another highly influential anti-personnel mine devised during the Cold War was the M14.

178
00:18:28,321 --> 00:18:35,320
This 1.5-inch high-pressure mine launched the era of small, cheap anti-personnel mines

179
00:18:35,321 --> 00:18:39,320
that could be mass-produced for a few dollars apiece.

180
00:18:39,321 --> 00:18:43,320
Nicknamed the Tow Popper because of its size and intended effect,

181
00:18:43,321 --> 00:18:49,320
it conspicuously turned the anti-personnel mine into a mine that was designed to maim.

182
00:18:49,321 --> 00:18:54,320
Made primarily of plastic, it was also extremely difficult to track down.

183
00:18:54,321 --> 00:19:00,320
It has essentially every evil property that a landmine can have.

184
00:19:00,321 --> 00:19:03,320
And because we made them, then the rest of the world thought,

185
00:19:03,321 --> 00:19:06,320
well, maybe that's a great idea. If they're doing it, we should do it too.

186
00:19:06,321 --> 00:19:10,320
So the Chinese made the Type 72A,

187
00:19:10,321 --> 00:19:15,320
and they've probably made more than 100 million of those, just a colossal number.

188
00:19:15,321 --> 00:19:26,829
And that mine has probably killed and injured more people than any other mine type in history, the Type 72A, which is a takeoff on the U.S.

189
00:19:26,830 --> 00:19:27,530
M14.

190
00:19:29,060 --> 00:19:33,840
The ingenuity behind the M14 had to do with its size and firing mechanism.

191
00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:43,100
When stepped on, a flexible convex plate called a Belleville spring would snap into reverse, driving a firing pin into the detonator.

192
00:19:43,101 --> 00:19:50,780
This action would ignite a friction-sensitive explosive in the detonator, which in turn set off the main charge.

193
00:19:51,860 --> 00:19:57,240
The tiny packet of 29 grams of tetral was just enough to mutilate a leg from the knee down.

194
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:03,380
If the global tensions of the 1950s spurred the development of efficient new mines,

195
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:11,640
the war in Vietnam would usher in another generation of mines with evil properties.

196
00:20:11,641 --> 00:20:15,620
mines that could be dropped by the thousands from the sky.

197
00:20:18,280 --> 00:20:24,360
Nearly 100 million landmines were cleared from Europe and North Africa between 1945 and 1949,

198
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:26,880
mostly by German POWs.

199
00:20:27,560 --> 00:20:29,640
Landmines will return on Modern Marvels.

200
00:20:35,060 --> 00:20:37,840
Mines played a bloody and pervasive role in Vietnam,

201
00:20:37,841 --> 00:20:43,520
where anti-personnel mines in particular were used by both sides in devastating new ways.

202
00:20:45,220 --> 00:20:49,980
Vietnam was an unconventional war with an enemy and a front that was hard to define.

203
00:20:51,740 --> 00:20:55,840
Hit-and-run guerrilla tactics made traditional mines and minefields obsolete.

204
00:20:56,280 --> 00:21:02,120
It wasn't like World War II where there was a front and the front moved forward or the front moved back.

205
00:21:02,121 --> 00:21:07,860
In Vietnam, it wasn't possible to predict where the conflict was going to be,

206
00:21:08,160 --> 00:21:11,760
which meant that where you didn't want the enemy to come today,

207
00:21:12,820 --> 00:21:14,800
you might want to be able to go tomorrow.

208
00:21:14,920 --> 00:21:17,940
Now, if you put down persistent landmines, you can't do that.

209
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:19,420
And we found that out the hard way.

210
00:21:19,421 --> 00:21:23,440
A lot of our own troops were injured or killed by those mines.

211
00:21:23,980 --> 00:21:25,640
And a lot of civilians, too.

212
00:21:25,641 --> 00:21:32,640
The North Vietnamese employed land mines in a radically new and aggressive way.

213
00:21:35,640 --> 00:21:39,640
Instead of using minefields to slow down or redirect enemy troops and tanks,

214
00:21:39,641 --> 00:21:44,640
they deployed mines as an offensive weapon, the primary aim of which was to inflict casualties.

215
00:21:44,641 --> 00:21:51,640
33% of all marine injuries and deaths in Vietnam were caused by mines.

216
00:21:55,640 --> 00:22:01,501
The North used Chinese and Soviet mines, but relied heavily on redeployed U.S.

217
00:22:01,502 --> 00:22:03,680
mines like this M16 Claymore.

218
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:08,120
And on mines they could fashion from the litter of the battlefield.

219
00:22:08,940 --> 00:22:13,980
They'd go out and if we dropped a bomb somewhere or some kind of a round,

220
00:22:14,540 --> 00:22:18,660
they were able to crack it open and take the explosive out

221
00:22:18,661 --> 00:22:21,220
and kind of melt it to where they could re-pour it

222
00:22:21,221 --> 00:22:26,700
and put it in a metal casing or they would put it in a mold form, wood.

223
00:22:27,460 --> 00:22:34,700
They were ingenious in how to make them go off as well as manufacture them.

224
00:22:34,701 --> 00:22:40,000
They camouflaged it to where you and I were not able to find it.

225
00:22:41,440 --> 00:22:43,520
American troops also deployed mines.

226
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:47,340
Some 80,000 claymores a month during the height of the war.

227
00:22:48,260 --> 00:22:51,560
But planting mines by hand left GIs vulnerable to sniper fire.

228
00:22:52,220 --> 00:22:56,980
And by the time a minefield was in place, it could be obsolete, the battle having moved on.

229
00:22:57,680 --> 00:23:02,380
To get around these problems, the U.S. came up with a revolutionary new kind of mine

230
00:23:02,381 --> 00:23:05,700
that could follow the flow of the battle, the scatterable.

231
00:23:06,460 --> 00:23:10,580
The blue .42 Dragon Tooth was even smaller and lighter than the Toe Popper.

232
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:17,340
Encased in hard plastic, it could be dropped by the thousands in minutes from the air.

233
00:23:18,560 --> 00:23:23,380
When stepped on, volatile chemicals in the 13-millimeter long mine mixed,

234
00:23:23,960 --> 00:23:25,860
setting off a blast that could tear off a foot.

235
00:23:25,861 --> 00:23:36,860
Scatterables were dumped over Laos, Cambodia and Vietnam in hope of crippling North Vietnamese supply lines.

236
00:23:36,861 --> 00:23:39,403
But the new mines had a major drawback.

237
00:23:39,404 --> 00:23:42,860
Their ease of deployment made them impossible to map.

238
00:23:42,861 --> 00:23:48,905
The era of carefully laid and recorded minefields was over, which meant that U.S.

239
00:23:48,906 --> 00:23:52,860
troops didn't always know where their own mines were.

240
00:23:52,861 --> 00:23:56,820
If you were smart, you really would hesitate about using those mines in Vietnam

241
00:23:56,821 --> 00:24:02,080
because you wouldn't know when your own troops might need to go through exactly that same area.

242
00:24:02,081 --> 00:24:07,380
But they wouldn't be able to without a very time-consuming mine breaching process.

243
00:24:08,140 --> 00:24:13,780
The Soviets quickly came out with their own scatterable, the PF-1 Butterfly Mine,

244
00:24:14,340 --> 00:24:16,680
so-called because of the way it floats to the ground.

245
00:24:18,520 --> 00:24:21,840
They deployed millions in the 1980s in Afghanistan.

246
00:24:22,860 --> 00:24:29,040
The humanitarian landmine crisis of today largely stems from the use of such mines.

247
00:24:32,240 --> 00:24:33,539
After Vietnam, the U.S.

248
00:24:33,540 --> 00:24:37,720
was determined to come up with a new, improved generation of scatterables.

249
00:24:38,460 --> 00:24:43,940
And in 1978, the family of scatterable mines, FASCAM for short, was introduced.

250
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:51,280
What made FASCAM mines different was their ability to self-destruct after a few hours or days,

251
00:24:51,281 --> 00:24:55,020
making them more suitable for the twists and turns of guerrilla warfare.

252
00:24:55,920 --> 00:24:57,420
The mines could be deployed by plane,

253
00:25:00,140 --> 00:25:00,840
artillery,

254
00:25:01,900 --> 00:25:04,620
or land vehicle.

255
00:25:07,340 --> 00:25:10,520
There was even a minefield and a briefcase for the foot soldier

256
00:25:10,521 --> 00:25:15,200
that could shoot out 17 anti-tank and 4 anti-personnel mines

257
00:25:15,201 --> 00:25:17,260
to a radius of 35 yards.

258
00:25:17,261 --> 00:25:25,260
Smart minds, as they were called, used simple digital timers, like those found in an ordinary wristwatch,

259
00:25:25,261 --> 00:25:30,260
that would complete an electric circuit after a few hours or days and explode the mine.

260
00:25:30,261 --> 00:25:39,260
Ironically, the humanitarian potential of minds that could turn themselves on and off and blow themselves up was not a factor in their design.

261
00:25:39,261 --> 00:25:46,880
The emergence of self-destructing minds was motivated entirely by military considerations.

262
00:25:47,840 --> 00:25:53,320
It's just serendipitous that they turned out to be an immense humanitarian advance.

263
00:25:53,540 --> 00:25:54,660
I mean, really immense.

264
00:25:55,300 --> 00:26:06,800
The lethal duration of a modern mind is about, on the average, about 64,000 times less than that of a traditional persistent mind.

265
00:26:06,801 --> 00:26:11,020
But it's unclear how reliable FASCAM mines are.

266
00:26:12,220 --> 00:26:16,120
Estimates for their failure rate range from 0.1 to 5%.

267
00:26:16,121 --> 00:26:25,600
More fundamentally, FASCAMs may actually perpetuate the use of dumb mines by poorer militaries and insurgent forces.

268
00:26:26,760 --> 00:26:30,720
Only advanced modern militaries are going to be able to afford to design, build, and fuel.

269
00:26:30,721 --> 00:26:39,000
So if advanced militaries get them, the lesser developed countries will want to retain long-lasting mines as a countervailing measure.

270
00:26:40,360 --> 00:26:48,740
Certainly rebel troops and small armies won't be able to afford vast quantities of the $120 atom, or area denial artillery mine,

271
00:26:49,060 --> 00:26:55,500
which can be dropped from the sky or shot out of a gun, and which self-deploys seven 20-foot tripwires upon landing.

272
00:26:55,501 --> 00:27:03,640
were the $114,000 wide area anti-armor munitions mine,

273
00:27:04,260 --> 00:27:05,780
otherwise known as WAM,

274
00:27:06,460 --> 00:27:10,000
a mine that uses multiple sensors and a penetrating warhead

275
00:27:10,001 --> 00:27:14,040
to detect and take out an enemy tank from a distance of 100 yards.

276
00:27:17,220 --> 00:27:22,340
Even greater distances and levels of lethality are routine for sea mines,

277
00:27:22,341 --> 00:27:26,140
which have also established themselves as a staple of modern war

278
00:27:26,141 --> 00:27:30,880
and spawned whole new forms of mine and countermine technology.

279
00:27:32,180 --> 00:27:36,740
During the Vietnam War, the U.S. dumped about 1,000 tons of gravel mines,

280
00:27:37,280 --> 00:27:41,140
anti-personnel scatterables about the size and shape of a sugar packet.

281
00:27:41,940 --> 00:27:43,940
Landmines will return on Modern Marvels.

282
00:27:48,060 --> 00:27:51,300
Mine warfare is not restricted to combat on land.

283
00:27:52,340 --> 00:27:57,340
Sea mines have also had a huge impact on 19th and 20th century conflicts.

284
00:27:57,341 --> 00:28:05,340
Indeed, sea mines have damaged and destroyed more ships than any other weapon in every war since the Civil War.

285
00:28:05,341 --> 00:28:12,340
The USS Roberts found out the hard way how devastating a single sea mine can be

286
00:28:12,341 --> 00:28:19,340
when it hit an Iranian M08 mine while patrolling the Persian Gulf on July 4th, 1989.

287
00:28:19,341 --> 00:28:22,180
This explosion was one of the most ferocious I'd ever seen.

288
00:28:23,500 --> 00:28:27,820
It actually blew the ship vertically about 15 or 20 feet in the air.

289
00:28:28,320 --> 00:28:34,400
A fireball went up from the after end of the ship that must have been somewhere between 100 and 150 feet.

290
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:39,380
I was standing 114 feet, 120 feet from the point of the explosion.

291
00:28:39,381 --> 00:28:40,600
It broke my left foot.

292
00:28:40,601 --> 00:28:50,320
The old-fashioned contact mine, packed with 250 pounds of TNT, nearly cracked the Roberts in half.

293
00:28:50,840 --> 00:28:52,900
The only thing holding it together was the deck.

294
00:28:53,800 --> 00:28:57,480
Interestingly, it wasn't the TNT directly that caused the enormous damage.

295
00:28:59,140 --> 00:29:04,200
A mine causes a balloon, almost a force balloon, under the ship.

296
00:29:04,720 --> 00:29:09,660
And that force balloon then comes up and bursts under the ship and lifts it up out of the water.

297
00:29:09,661 --> 00:29:19,820
And when it releases the pressure, it's the falling back into the water after being lifted that causes the ship to break its back and usually go right to the bottom.

298
00:29:21,500 --> 00:29:30,180
Visions of such spectacular destruction in which a cheap explosive wipes out the enemy's most expensive ship inspired the development of sea mines from the start.

299
00:29:30,181 --> 00:29:37,240
David Bushnell, an American student at Yale who invented the submarine during the Revolutionary War,

300
00:29:37,840 --> 00:29:40,080
also designed and built the first sea mine.

301
00:29:41,680 --> 00:29:47,820
Known as a keg mine, this simple contact mine was made by packing a wooden butter keg with gunpowder

302
00:29:47,821 --> 00:29:49,380
and attaching it to a float.

303
00:29:50,220 --> 00:29:54,540
A hammer and plintlock mechanism, like that on a musket, was attached to the mine,

304
00:29:54,800 --> 00:29:56,500
which was triggered when hit by a ship.

305
00:29:56,501 --> 00:30:01,885
The mines failed to knock out any British vessels. Their only victims?

306
00:30:01,886 --> 00:30:05,500
Two boys who tried to fish out one of the kegs.

307
00:30:05,501 --> 00:30:16,500
Water mines proved much more effective in the Civil War when the South used them as a great equalizer against the North, sinking or damaging some 43 Union ships.

308
00:30:16,501 --> 00:30:25,500
The South offered half the cargo of a sunken ship to anyone who could design an effective mine, or torpedo as they were then called.

309
00:30:25,501 --> 00:30:29,000
Some of the designs were little better than the keg mine.

310
00:30:29,001 --> 00:30:33,000
They actually used a container that was originally intended for whiskey.

311
00:30:33,001 --> 00:30:36,000
A five gallon container packed it with black gunpowder,

312
00:30:36,001 --> 00:30:40,000
put an artillery friction piece in it, and then suspended it below a float.

313
00:30:40,001 --> 00:30:46,000
And one of the Union vessels stumbled upon it, went down in 30 feet of water.

314
00:30:46,001 --> 00:30:48,000
The only thing showing was the smokestacks.

315
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,000
Not all mines were so rudimentary.

316
00:30:54,001 --> 00:31:00,000
The Singer Mine, a moored contact mine invented by the brother of the sewing machine magnate,

317
00:31:00,001 --> 00:31:07,000
used a spring-driven plunger that set off a 60-pound black powder charge.

318
00:31:07,001 --> 00:31:11,000
A more radical innovation was the introduction of electrical fuses.

319
00:31:11,001 --> 00:31:15,000
They actually had electrical controllers on the shore.

320
00:31:15,001 --> 00:31:23,000
When the ship passed closest to the mine, then a well-hidden shore contingent would set the switch and cause the mine to detonate.

321
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:31,000
The laying of mines on the open sea didn't begin until the 20th century, most dramatically

322
00:31:31,660 --> 00:31:33,919
in the First World War, when the U.S.

323
00:31:33,920 --> 00:31:37,400
and British planted 72,000 mines along the North Atlantic

324
00:31:37,401 --> 00:31:41,323
coast in an attempt to bottle up the German U-boat.

325
00:31:41,324 --> 00:31:44,400
The North Sea Barrage, as it was called,

326
00:31:44,401 --> 00:31:47,256
was the largest underwater minefield ever deployed.

327
00:31:47,257 --> 00:31:49,160
It featured an innovative new mine

328
00:31:49,161 --> 00:31:54,760
developed by the U.S., called the MK-6, that used a floating copper wire to extend the

329
00:31:54,761 --> 00:31:57,319
contact range of the mine by 300%.

330
00:31:57,320 --> 00:32:01,760
Any submarine that grazed the wire would trigger the mine's

331
00:32:01,761 --> 00:32:03,020
300-pound charge.

332
00:32:03,021 --> 00:32:08,060
Late in the last year of the war, the spectacular blockade destroyed

333
00:32:08,061 --> 00:32:13,160
at least six German subs and forced the rest of the U-boat fleet into time-consuming evasive

334
00:32:13,161 --> 00:32:18,340
maneuvers. Sea mines played a more pervasive and lethal

335
00:32:18,341 --> 00:32:24,560
role in World War II, in large part because they were more sophisticated and deadly.

336
00:32:24,561 --> 00:32:29,780
Old-fashioned contact mines were displaced by stealthier influence mines, which could

337
00:32:29,781 --> 00:32:34,280
now be dropped from the air, could lurk at the bottom of the sea, and were triggered

338
00:32:34,281 --> 00:32:37,093
by magnetic or acoustic sensors.

339
00:32:37,094 --> 00:32:42,280
The sensors could detect the mere noise or magnetic charge,

340
00:32:42,281 --> 00:32:47,240
or change in water pressure caused by a ship passing hundreds of feet above, thereby setting

341
00:32:47,241 --> 00:32:48,251
off the mine.

342
00:32:48,252 --> 00:32:54,240
Influenced mines were much harder to sweep and took a vast toll on all sides.

343
00:32:55,280 --> 00:33:01,660
The Germans and Japanese alone lost some 2,700 ships to mines, one for every 37 mines that

344
00:33:01,661 --> 00:33:02,465
were laid.

345
00:33:02,466 --> 00:33:08,660
As with land mines, sea mines posed a major problem on D-Day, and the largest

346
00:33:09,420 --> 00:33:13,960
minesweeping armada ever assembled cleared mines out of the English Channel on the night

347
00:33:13,961 --> 00:33:18,080
of June 5th, just prior to the attack.

348
00:33:18,081 --> 00:33:26,760
There were some 250 ships in the lead of the D-Day armada that were dedicated to clearing

349
00:33:26,761 --> 00:33:28,350
the lanes across the channel.

350
00:33:28,351 --> 00:33:31,860
The first casualties of that operation occurred with the loss of

351
00:33:31,861 --> 00:33:34,740
minesweepers.

352
00:33:34,741 --> 00:33:39,180
The minesweeping ships dragged cable cutters through the channel to slice through the lines

353
00:33:39,181 --> 00:33:43,960
that held the moored mines in place. They also towed electrical cables that could

354
00:33:43,961 --> 00:33:48,960
simulate the magnetic charge of a passing ship and thereby detonate magnetic mines.

355
00:33:48,961 --> 00:33:55,340
The effort that we went through cutting the cables to the mines and allowing our gunboats

356
00:33:55,341 --> 00:34:02,760
to sink them when they surfaced was a tremendous effort and had to proceed any landing to prevent

357
00:34:02,761 --> 00:34:06,280
serious loss.

358
00:34:06,281 --> 00:34:09,960
Mine sweeping has come a long way since the Second World War.

359
00:34:09,961 --> 00:34:13,960
Much of the work is now done by the Navy's MH-53E helicopters,

360
00:34:13,961 --> 00:34:17,960
which are strong enough to drag mine detectors and cable cutters through the waves

361
00:34:17,961 --> 00:34:21,960
and can do so more quickly and safely than ships.

362
00:34:21,961 --> 00:34:25,960
Divers still have to hunt down the hard-to-find mines,

363
00:34:25,961 --> 00:34:29,960
but a whole new generation of high-tech mine hunting tools

364
00:34:29,961 --> 00:34:33,960
is about to emerge, or submerge.

365
00:34:33,961 --> 00:34:37,960
We're trying to get people out of the loop from dealing with

366
00:34:37,961 --> 00:34:39,135
the mine problem.

367
00:34:39,136 --> 00:34:44,040
So we're concentrating a great deal of effort on using unmanned systems

368
00:34:44,041 --> 00:34:53,920
or basically robots to help with this mine problem. Unmanned underwater vehicles are

369
00:34:53,921 --> 00:34:58,860
likely to be the minesweepers of the future, supplementing operations from ship and air.

370
00:35:00,640 --> 00:35:06,120
Equipped with cameras, sophisticated sensors, and robot appendages, some of the UUVs are

371
00:35:06,121 --> 00:35:08,727
programmed to find and recover mines.

372
00:35:08,728 --> 00:35:12,180
Others carry explosives, which they drop on mines

373
00:35:12,181 --> 00:35:14,345
at the bottom of the sea.

374
00:35:14,346 --> 00:35:19,540
For shallow water sweeps, crawlers that look like mini tanks

375
00:35:19,541 --> 00:35:23,320
are engineered to handle the complex dynamics of breaking waves.

376
00:35:28,040 --> 00:35:33,840
The Rapid Airborne Mine Clearance System, or RAMEX, meanwhile, may provide another interesting

377
00:35:33,841 --> 00:35:38,760
way of getting rid of underwater mines by literally shooting them into extinction with

378
00:35:38,761 --> 00:35:42,436
bullets designed to stay on course after they hit the water.

379
00:35:42,437 --> 00:35:44,580
It does that by having a projectile

380
00:35:44,581 --> 00:35:50,080
that creates its own cavity as it passes through the water so that it isn't slowed down, okay,

381
00:35:50,180 --> 00:35:53,295
by the water like a conventional bullet.

382
00:35:53,296 --> 00:35:57,500
Getting rid of mines in the surf zone is a job for the

383
00:35:57,501 --> 00:36:02,700
Navy's experimental explosive nets and line charges, which use blast pressure to detonate

384
00:36:02,701 --> 00:36:09,700
mines.

385
00:36:10,080 --> 00:36:15,620
Unfortunately, such heavy-handed methods can't eliminate 100% of the mines.

386
00:36:15,621 --> 00:36:19,640
And while that's okay for clearing a path in combat, it's worthless when it comes to

387
00:36:19,641 --> 00:36:23,940
reclaiming land for civilian use.

388
00:36:23,941 --> 00:36:27,500
The humanitarian demining kit of the 21st century

389
00:36:27,501 --> 00:36:30,900
requires an altogether different set of mine hunting tools.

390
00:36:33,100 --> 00:36:36,640
Most landmines cost between $3 and $30 to make,

391
00:36:37,060 --> 00:36:39,960
and between $300 and $1,000 to clear.

392
00:36:41,640 --> 00:36:44,000
Landmines will return on Modern Marvels.

393
00:36:47,260 --> 00:36:51,940
Putting landmines on or in the ground is a whole lot easier than getting them out,

394
00:36:52,600 --> 00:36:58,600
Especially after a war, when an area has to be totally demined in order to be ready for civilian use.

395
00:37:00,360 --> 00:37:05,220
Would you send your children to play soccer in a field that they said, we think we got 99% of them?

396
00:37:05,640 --> 00:37:06,540
Absolutely not.

397
00:37:08,940 --> 00:37:13,300
Adding to the challenge is the fact that deminers face a mind-boggling range of mines,

398
00:37:13,980 --> 00:37:16,640
including many that are booby-trapped to explode when lifted.

399
00:37:16,641 --> 00:37:17,341
The U.S.

400
00:37:17,342 --> 00:37:29,780
State Department's humanitarian demining program puts out a pack of 52 mine cards per country, like this one for Bosnia, that alert deminers to major mines.

401
00:37:31,920 --> 00:37:39,880
The best post-conflict demining tool after the metal detector and the probe, which have been in use since World War II, is a minesweeping dog.

402
00:37:39,881 --> 00:37:51,153
Hundreds of Belgian and German Shepherds, trained to smell explosives and immediately signal a mine, are at work in the minefields of Europe, Africa, Asia, and South

403
00:37:51,154 --> 00:37:51,854
America.

404
00:37:52,860 --> 00:37:59,980
Demining dogs cost around $16,000, however, and can't work in wind, which confuses their sense of scent.

405
00:38:01,600 --> 00:38:08,720
Another critical part of the demining toolkit that is making life safer for those on the front lines is personal protection equipment.

406
00:38:08,721 --> 00:38:12,680
like this full body suit designed by med engineers in Canada

407
00:38:12,681 --> 00:38:17,280
that was blast tested against a full range of anti-personnel mines.

408
00:38:21,300 --> 00:38:25,420
If you're not wearing any PPE and a mine goes off, you're going to be dismembered.

409
00:38:25,920 --> 00:38:29,280
But if you're wearing PPE, upper body protection, lower body protection,

410
00:38:29,281 --> 00:38:33,680
maybe foot protection, hopefully you're going to minimize the injury enough

411
00:38:33,681 --> 00:38:36,700
so that the medical attention that's there can save your life

412
00:38:36,701 --> 00:38:39,200
and maybe give you something to walk on.

413
00:38:40,460 --> 00:38:45,920
Unfortunately, blast-resistant visors, vests, and boots cost thousands of dollars

414
00:38:45,921 --> 00:38:49,340
and are too expensive for most deminers in the third world.

415
00:38:52,860 --> 00:38:57,440
Also prohibitively expensive for much of the world are the latest demining vehicles,

416
00:38:58,000 --> 00:38:59,900
spin-offs of the old rollers and plows,

417
00:39:00,120 --> 00:39:02,740
like this 56-ton Rhino Earth Tiller,

418
00:39:02,741 --> 00:39:07,120
which dredges and detonates mines by crushing them between its upper and lower drums.

419
00:39:09,040 --> 00:39:13,760
Or this floating mine blade, a remotely operated bulldozer with teeth

420
00:39:13,761 --> 00:39:17,600
that dig 10 inches into the ground and separate earth from mine.

421
00:39:19,800 --> 00:39:24,080
Aerial mine clearing is a promising technology that might be available in a few years.

422
00:39:25,980 --> 00:39:31,800
A camcopter in development by the U.S. Army uses onboard GPS and infrared sensors

423
00:39:31,801 --> 00:39:34,500
that can read the heat signature of a landmine

424
00:39:34,501 --> 00:39:38,400
and thereby create a digital mine map of a given piece of land.

425
00:39:39,320 --> 00:39:42,420
The map can then be used by deminers to track down mines.

426
00:39:44,420 --> 00:39:47,560
Still more creative approaches include the use of bees,

427
00:39:48,300 --> 00:39:51,500
equipped with tiny radio backs and trained to scent out mines,

428
00:39:52,300 --> 00:39:54,360
and something called purple grass,

429
00:39:54,361 --> 00:39:57,300
a weed that would change color when close to a mine.

430
00:39:59,600 --> 00:40:01,040
Despite these prospects,

431
00:40:01,800 --> 00:40:07,200
Experts say it is highly unlikely that we'll ever develop a technology that makes demining easy and safe.

432
00:40:07,860 --> 00:40:11,120
There are so many different mines, so many different types of soil,

433
00:40:11,460 --> 00:40:13,520
so many different environmental circumstances,

434
00:40:13,980 --> 00:40:17,680
that there is no one silver bullet that's going to solve the problem at once.

435
00:40:19,560 --> 00:40:21,860
The problem, of course, will never be solved

436
00:40:21,861 --> 00:40:25,520
as long as people keep putting more mines into the ground than they can take out.

437
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:30,080
This still appears to be what's happening.

438
00:40:30,081 --> 00:40:36,080
Humanitarian deminers have cleared nearly 3,500 square miles of land worldwide.

439
00:40:36,081 --> 00:40:45,080
But feuding countries with bitter border disputes, like Pakistan and India, have deployed millions of new mines.

440
00:40:45,081 --> 00:40:51,080
Meanwhile, the civilian casualty count from post-war mines continues to mount.

441
00:40:51,081 --> 00:40:55,080
15,000 to 20,000 new victims every year.

442
00:40:55,081 --> 00:41:00,740
For obvious reasons, that's a reality that's hard for most Americans to really understand.

443
00:41:00,920 --> 00:41:11,060
I think all it would take is a few landmines, God forbid, planted on town squares and commons in New England or the Midwest to drive it home.

444
00:41:11,540 --> 00:41:15,740
That we need to ban this awful, insidious weapon of mass destruction.

445
00:41:16,700 --> 00:41:22,020
Jerry White lost his right leg when he stepped on a landmine while visiting Israel as a student in 1984.

446
00:41:22,860 --> 00:41:28,000
The mine had been laid 17 years earlier during the 1967 Arab-Israeli War.

447
00:41:28,420 --> 00:41:34,820
One beautiful spring day in 1984, I was hiking down the side of a hillside,

448
00:41:34,821 --> 00:41:38,480
and boom, the world exploded all around me.

449
00:41:39,040 --> 00:41:43,020
My lower right leg had been blown off and was rather shredded,

450
00:41:43,300 --> 00:41:46,940
and my left leg was blown open with my bones showing,

451
00:41:46,941 --> 00:41:54,900
and in fact, bones from one of my feet had become arrows into my second leg.

452
00:41:55,940 --> 00:42:00,060
White was told that stepping on a mine was an anomaly, a freak occurrence.

453
00:42:00,660 --> 00:42:05,100
But the more he investigated, the more he discovered that his experience was far from rare.

454
00:42:06,160 --> 00:42:09,320
It wasn't just Israel. That wasn't the most mine-affected country in the world.

455
00:42:09,460 --> 00:42:12,000
It was Bosnia, it was Cambodia, it was Angola.

456
00:42:12,001 --> 00:42:17,160
It was all these countries that were in the middle of civil war or emerging from civil war,

457
00:42:17,280 --> 00:42:22,680
where you saw massive casualties, hundreds per month in some cases.

458
00:42:23,120 --> 00:42:26,080
So it was really taking and doing the math.

459
00:42:28,700 --> 00:42:31,940
White, co-founder of the Landmine Survivors Network,

460
00:42:32,620 --> 00:42:37,740
has been part of a humanitarian grassroots movement to ban anti-personnel landmines worldwide.

461
00:42:37,741 --> 00:42:46,740
Launched in 1992, the international campaign has achieved unprecedented success as an initiative

462
00:42:46,741 --> 00:42:49,740
fueled entirely by non-governmental organizations.

463
00:42:51,740 --> 00:42:56,340
To date, 134 countries have signed the landmark treaty.

464
00:42:56,740 --> 00:43:00,540
The United States, Russia, and China have not.

465
00:43:02,240 --> 00:43:07,400
These indiscriminate killers constitute one of the greatest public health hazards.

466
00:43:07,401 --> 00:43:13,100
Royal celebrities like Queen Noor of Jordan and Princess Diana before her death in 1997

467
00:43:13,101 --> 00:43:17,920
brought massive attention to an issue that struck a humanitarian chord worldwide.

468
00:43:20,700 --> 00:43:23,500
Even Superman and Wonder Woman have done their part,

469
00:43:24,080 --> 00:43:27,160
warning children in third world countries about the dangers of mines.

470
00:43:28,280 --> 00:43:34,680
I think the success of the movement of inland mines is largely based on a visceral understanding

471
00:43:34,681 --> 00:43:38,780
that a weapon planted and hidden in the ground

472
00:43:38,781 --> 00:43:43,300
that is meant to maim and rip off body parts

473
00:43:43,301 --> 00:43:49,160
and does this to children and women and innocent civilians every single day

474
00:43:49,161 --> 00:43:51,480
is just plain wrong.

475
00:43:51,481 --> 00:44:05,481
Weapons of war, of course, are supposed to maim and rip off body parts,

476
00:44:06,160 --> 00:44:17,160
which is precisely why landmines will probably continue to wreak havoc in war and beyond war for many years to come.

477
00:44:17,161 --> 00:44:24,960
Some heroes with the right stuff never left the ground.

478
00:44:25,460 --> 00:44:29,480
If we didn't come up with some answers, this crew might not come back to Earth.

479
00:44:29,660 --> 00:44:31,680
Failure is not an option.

480
00:44:31,681 --> 00:44:36,300
Sunday night, August 24th at 9, only on the History Channel.

481
00:44:37,100 --> 00:44:38,920
Even astronauts have heroes.

